               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="130X/131X">s. xiv in</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_420">Oxford, Oxfordshire. Franciscan
                        convent. </orgName><p>Two bifolia (the bottom half of one bifolium and a
                        nearly complete second bifolium) representing the penultimate stage in the
                        making of the Tabula septem custodiarum; it preserves references to the
                        works of Pope Gregory the Great rearranged in the order of the Bible text.
                        Presumed to have been written at the Oxford Greyfriars, where this and the
                        Registrum Anglie were compiled. An Oxford provenance is buttressed by the
                        fact that these leaves became binding scrap in Oxford, acquired by the
                        Oxford antiquary Philip Bliss (1787--1857). Illustrated, Rouse and Rouse,
                        Registrum Anglie, CBMLC 2 (1991), fig. 4 (p. clxix).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Philip Bliss (1787--1857),
                     antiquary.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold with Bliss's collection of binders'
                     scrap to Sir Thomas Phillipps, part of Phillipps MS 18133.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Quaritch, acquired by Prof. Ralph Hanna III,
                     by whom given to Richard Rouse (co-editor of Registrum Anglie).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The Rouse collection was presented to UCLA by
                     Mary and Richard Rouse in 2005 and 2011.</provenance>
               </history>
